# Hover Racer directional thrusters

Reopen the project after input-map changes, then run Main with F6. Preserve any unsaved editor work separately before reloading external edits.

Both sticks point the ship toward the requested direction across the full circle, then drive it forward along its heading. Up means ahead; left/right means turn the nose left/right and travel that way; down means turn around and travel behind. Down no longer means nose-fixed reverse. Magnitude controls thrust with a radial deadzone of 0.18. Trigger propulsion and the previous small horizontal steering assist are removed.

The heading reference is captured from the ship/follow camera when a gesture begins. It stays fixed while either stick remains deflected: holding left reaches a leftward heading and stays there, even as the camera follows the turn. Centre BOTH sticks to coast and refresh the reference; the next up gesture means forward from the new facing. Moving a held stick around its circle updates the target within that fixed frame.

The two independent stick vectors blend to a target direction. One active stick supplies half the combined propulsion. Near-opposite inputs blend into differential turning instead of following an unstable near-zero target: left up/right down spins right, inverse spins left. Equal opposed horizontal inputs cancel. Neutral cuts powered turning/thrust and preserves drifting momentum. Powered grip redirects movement toward the nose. Bank, stern swing and skipping remain visual only.

Keyboard equivalents: WASD is the left stick; arrow keys are the right stick. A+Left commands left; D+Right commands right; W+Up drives ahead; S+Down turns around and drives behind. W+Down spins right; S+Up spins left. Y/R resets. Individual exhaust indicates each engine's strength; cyan rear exhaust is forward drive, orange front exhaust shows opposed counter-thrust.

The saved course remains approximately 918 metres long and 28 metres wide, with six rounded corners, a dogleg, and hills rising 10 and 14 metres. Hover suspension samples actual road collisions, with tiny fallback samples for triangle-edge ray misses. Falling off the course resets the ship.

Tunable Ship exports: top speed 52 m/s, acceleration 145 m/s², bounded turn speed 1.8 rad/s, heading response 6, bank up to 36 degrees, thrust grip 6.5, hover response 22. Speed and acceleration increased from 40 and 110. FOV changes smoothly by at most 8 degrees; mesh animation does not tilt/bob the camera or collider.

Run the checks from this project directory:

```
Godot --headless --fixed-fps 60 --path . --script res://tests/controller_input.gd
Godot --headless --fixed-fps 60 --path . --script res://tests/hill_course.gd
```

The control test uses simulated controller events and checks actual facing and travel on a large isolated collision floor. It covers held directions, direction changes without recentering, reference refresh, mixed/opposed vectors, radial analog response, coast, keyboard, reset and cap. The hill test checks the actual course, barriers and full-speed hill following. Neither judges physical controller feel or visual comfort.

`tools/build_course.gd` regenerates TestTrack.tscn and course_samples.json, replacing manual course edits. Back up before running it. Files before this control update are preserved in `backups/before-full-circle-heading` with `.bak` suffixes.
